  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Dakota has received $545M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 2,921 awards with an average award size of $187K. Obligations fell 32.3% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows variation: FY21 totaled $624M, FY22 totaled $1.2B, FY23 totaled $1B, FY24 totaled $1.3B, FY25 totaled $804M, and FY26 totaled $545M so far. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in FY2026 to date at $242M, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $107M and the Department of the Interior at $92.4M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads industries at $134M, followed by Computer Facilities Management Services at $68.2M and Electric Power Distribution at $33.3M. KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC is the top vendor in FY2026 to date at $67.9M, followed by CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC at $41.8M and FEDERAL CONTRACTING INC at $40.4M. All figures are contract obligations and FY2026 totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting trended in South Dakota over recent years?",
        "body": "Federal obligations rose from $624M in FY21 to $1.2B in FY22, then moved to $1B in FY23 and $1.3B in FY24 before falling to $804M in FY25. FY26 obligations are $545M to date. The year-over-year change for FY2026 to date is down 32.3%."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors account for the most federal dollars in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $242M in South Dakota in FY2026 to date, the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $107M, and the Department of the Interior obligated $92.4M. KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC received $67.9M, CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC received $41.8M, and FEDERAL CONTRACTING INC received $40.4M in FY2026 to date."
      }
    ]
  },
